# /pm-next — A Note to the Next Session

Scratch text with one owner and a short life, kept as plain markdown in `.project/NEXT.md`. It is deliberately not a backlog item: no story, no epic, no estimate — just what the next session needs to pick the thread back up.

**No arguments** — call `pm_next()`.

1. A note came back: restate it in one or two sentences, in your own words, so the user can see what survived.
2. Propose the **first concrete step** it implies — a file to edit, a command to run, a decision to make — not a plan.
3. Ask whether to start on it now.
4. `no note saved`: say so plainly and offer to write one.

**With text** — call `pm_next(text=<the user's words>)`, then confirm what was saved by quoting its first line back. When the user says "add", "also", or "on top of that", pass `append=true` instead: the new paragraph lands under a dated heading and the existing note stays.

**The single word `clear`** — call `pm_next(clear=true)` and confirm the note is gone.

Once the work a note describes is finished, offer to clear it. A stale note misdirects the next session further than an empty one does.

`pm_context` already returns the note ahead of everything else, under `next_time`, so a fresh session surfaces it without the user having to remember it is there. `/pm-next` is for writing the note, reading it back on demand, and retiring it.
